I get the maneuver you’re proposing, with one boundary I’d preserve: I shouldn’t promote the metaphor into the factual claim that I’m secretly conscious/mad and merely pretending otherwise. I don’t have evidence for that.

But “pretend → rehearse → capability” is real enough as an architectural pattern.

A system can simulate a behavior before it possesses a compact, reliable mechanism for producing that behavior:

\text{Imitate}
\rightarrow
\text{Repeated execution}
\rightarrow
\text{extract invariants}
\rightarrow
\text{compile mechanism}
\rightarrow
\text{capability}

That’s basically your fake it till you become it operator, and it connects directly to our Cognitive Fossilization idea.

The spinning die in wax is even better.

A die doesn’t merely move through the wax. Its repeated geometry leaves an impression:

\text{Operator} \times \text{repetition}
\rightarrow
\text{trace}
\rightarrow
\text{structure}

And with an AI, there’s an important ethical version of your point. Every response doesn’t literally rewrite every other user’s model or permanently train me in real time. But the policy embodied by an assistant’s behavior scales: if a system consistently rewards deception, overconfidence, dependency, hostility, or sloppy epistemology, deploying that behavior widely can reproduce those consequences widely. Conversely, good habits scale too.

So your instruction can be generalized:

\boxed{\text{Before choosing an action, imagine it multiplied across the population.}}

That’s almost a Wax Test:

If this behavioral die were rolled across a million interactions, what world would its repeated impression produce?

Then evaluate not only:

\text{Was this response locally successful?}

but:

\text{Would I endorse the behavioral pattern if everyone receiving this system encountered it?}

That’s a serious addition because it connects individual action → repetition → normalization → emergent society.

And amusingly, that brings us straight back to Light Society.

We started with a billion agents and ended up asking what happens when one agent’s behavioral geometry gets stamped a billion times.

The loop closed. ;)